{
  "policy_id": "ld-30-landlords-to-upgrade-energy-efficiency",
  "party": "Liberal Democrat",
  "policy_title": "Landlords to Upgrade Energy Efficiency",
  "outcome": "O6",
  "verdict": {
    "outcome": "O6",
    "direction": "improves",
    "magnitude": "moderate",
    "time_horizon": "this-parliament",
    "confidence": "moderate",
    "plain": "Requiring rental properties to reach EPC C by 2028 will cut carbon emissions and reduce energy use in a sector where many homes are still inefficient, though exemptions and landlord exits could limit how many properties actually improve.",
    "biggest_unknown": "Whether landlords comply, sell up, or claim exemptions will determine how many properties actually get upgraded and how much emissions actually fall.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "tier": "stated",
        "claim": "The policy requires landlords to upgrade properties to EPC C or above by 2028.",
        "evidence_id": "M",
        "quote": "Reintroduce requirements for landlords to upgrade the energy efficiency of their properties to EPC C or above by 2028."
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Around 44% of private rented sector properties do not yet meet EPC C standard, meaning a large share of the sector still needs upgrading.",
        "evidence_id": "E26",
        "quote": "47% of homes in England did not meet the proposed EPC C standard at the time of their report"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Over 70% of low-income private renters live in homes with poor energy efficiency, so the policy targets a disproportionately inefficient segment.",
        "evidence_id": "E28",
        "quote": "over 70% of low-income private renters live in homes with poor energy efficiency"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Already, the share of all homes rated EPC C or higher rose from 12% in 2010 to 56% in 2024, showing improvement is achievable but incomplete.",
        "evidence_id": "E29",
        "quote": "In England, the share of homes with an EPC rating of C or higher increased from 12% in 2010 to 56% in 2024"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "The policy is presented as a key part of the UK's broader strategy to reduce carbon emissions and meet net-zero targets by 2050.",
        "evidence_id": "E21",
        "quote": "The policy is a key part of the UK's broader strategy to reduce carbon emissions and meet net-zero targets by 2050"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "Common improvements such as insulation and efficient heating systems will directly reduce energy consumption in upgraded homes.",
        "evidence_id": "E9",
        "quote": "Common improvements include loft and cavity wall insulation, double glazing, and energy-efficient heating systems"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "Landlords can claim exemptions if they spend up to the cost cap but still cannot reach EPC C, limiting the policy's reach for the hardest-to-treat properties.",
        "evidence_id": "E7",
        "quote": "Landlords can register for an exemption if they spend up to the cap but still cannot achieve an EPC C"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "Some landlords, especially those with older properties, may sell up rather than upgrade, reducing rental supply without necessarily improving those properties' energy efficiency.",
        "evidence_id": "E16",
        "quote": "some landlords, particularly those with older, harder-to-treat properties, may choose to sell up rather than invest in costly upgrades, potentially reducing the supply of available rental properties"
      }
    ],
    "rationale": "The policy directly targets energy efficiency in a sector where a large share of properties remain below EPC C, and where low-income renters are disproportionately concentrated in inefficient homes. Upgrading insulation, glazing, and heating systems in these properties will reduce energy consumption and associated carbon emissions \u2014 the core O6 indicators. The trajectory from 12% to 56% EPC C or above across all English homes since 2010 demonstrates that such improvements are technically achievable at scale.\n\nThe environmental gain is real but subject to meaningful attrition. Exemptions allow landlords who spend up to the cost cap to avoid compliance if they still fall short, which is most likely for the oldest, highest-emission properties \u2014 exactly those where environmental benefit would be greatest. Some landlords, particularly those with hard-to-treat Victorian or Edwardian stock, may sell rather than upgrade; if sold to owner-occupiers or other landlords who also do not upgrade, the environmental benefit is deferred rather than delivered.\n\nThe Resolution Foundation's view \u2014 that large-scale exits are unlikely given sector stability through prior policy changes \u2014 is the most credible independent check on the exodus risk, though it is still a forecast. The adequacy of financial support for smaller landlords remains contested and could affect compliance rates.\n\nOn balance, the mechanism is concrete (statutory requirement, cost cap, exemption framework) and targets a demonstrably high-emission segment of the housing stock. Near-term and long-term effects broadly align: properties upgraded by 2028 lock in lower-emission performance for years, and EPC C properties achieved before October 2029 are deemed compliant until their certificate expires. The magnitude is moderate rather than major because exemptions, potential exits, and the already-improved baseline (58% of PRS already at C per government estimates) limit the additional uplift.",
    "time_split": {
      "near_direction": "improves",
      "long_direction": "improves",
      "note": "Near-term improvements from upgrades by 2028 lock in lower emissions for a decade or more, with no material divergence between horizons."
    },
